        '6-8', $q$How Honeybees Help Farms$q$, $q$(1) Many people think of honeybees only as insects that make honey. (2) However, bees do another job that matters even more to farmers. (3) Pollination is the movement of pollen from one flower to another so that the plant can make seeds and fruit. (4) When a honeybee lands on a blossom to drink nectar, tiny grains of pollen stick to the hairs on its body. (5) As the bee visits the next flower, some of that pollen rubs off.

(6) Many crops depend on this process. (7) Apple trees, for example, produce very little fruit unless insects carry pollen between their blossoms. (8) Almonds, blueberries, and cucumbers also rely on visiting bees. (9) My cousin likes cucumbers in her salad. (10) Some farmers even rent hives and place them in their orchards during the spring bloom.

(11) Honeybee colonies face problems such as disease, harsh weather, and a shortage of wildflowers. (12) Researchers and beekeepers are working together to understand these threats and protect healthy hives.$q$, 'draft')
